![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

There's a bug in the error handling for the callback server. If the server fails to start (e.g., port is in use), the `promise` is rejected and this `catch` block is executed. However, `callbackServer` remains non-null.

Later, if the user presses Enter without pasting a code, the logic on line 1077 will be true, and it will attempt to `await Promise.race([callbackServer.promise, ...])`. Since `callbackServer.promise` has already been rejected, `Promise.race` will reject immediately, causing the authorization to fail unexpectedly instead of allowing for a manual fallback.

To fix this, you should set `callbackServer` to `null` within this `catch` block. This will correctly signal that the automatic capture mechanism is not available, ensuring the logic gracefully falls back to requiring manual code entry.

```javascript
              .catch(() => {
                /* timeout or error — user can paste manually */
                callbackServer = null;
              });
```

